planning and parallel

387
00:23:48,190 --> 00:23:50,590
execution of those
plans and how we

388
00:23:50,590 --> 00:23:55,830
can use the model itself to
enable it to think better

389
00:23:55,830 --> 00:23:59,790
in parallel, and we
can optimize that.

390
00:23:59,790 --> 00:24:04,990
So this paper, SPRINT, was
motivated by this observation

391
00:24:04,990 --> 00:24:09,070
that the reasoning
models such as o1, Gemini

392
00:24:09,070 --> 00:24:14,150
Think, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and
pretty much all the frontier

393
00:24:14,150 --> 00:24:19,820
models right now, they have
this tendency that for harder

394
00:24:19,820 --> 00:24:22,020
problems, they think more.

395
00:24:22,020 --> 00:24:26,560
And this longer thinking
corresponds to higher accuracy,

396
00:24:26,560 --> 00:24:30,620
so these models to think
for longer and longer

397
00:24:30,620 --> 00:24:32,220
like chain-of-thoughts.

398
00:24:32,220 --> 00:24:34,580
For example, these
two graphs here

399
00:24:34,580 --> 00:24:38,460
show that the DeepSeek-R1
during the training process.

400
00:24:38,460 --> 00:24:41,500
As the training
process continues,

401
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:43,540
obviously, it becomes
better at solving

402
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:46,080
this, Amy, these math problems.

403
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:50,820
But also, if we take a look at
the average length per response,

404
00:24:50,820 --> 00:24:52,900
that is also going
up, so it seems

405
00:24:52,900 --> 00:24:55,380
like there is a correlation
between model thinking

406
00:24:55,380 --> 00:24:58,020
for longer and a
larger sum of number

407
00:24:58,020 --> 00:25:01,900
of tokens and the accuracy
that we get from that.

408
00:25:01,900 --> 00:25:04,980
Now, again, so the
observation here

409
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:11,040
was that these long
reasoning steps are helpful,

410
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:14,100
but at the same time, many
of these reasoning steps

411
00:25:14,100 --> 00:25:18,930
seem to be independent
of each other.

412
00:25:18,930 --> 00:25:22,390
For example, the model tries
alternative approaches,

413
00:25:22,390 --> 00:25:25,730
it decomposes a task into
subtasks that some of them

414
00:25:25,730 --> 00:25:29,410
can be run in parallel, and
then some of these steps

415
00:25:29,410 --> 00:25:31,870
are verifying the
previous steps.

416
00:25:31,870 --> 00:25:35,050
But there is a lot of-- if
we look into the graph that

417
00:25:35,050 --> 00:25:40,370
describes the
reasoning trace, there

418
00:25:40,370 --> 00:25:42,570
are parts of these
computations that

419
00:25:42,570 --> 00:25:44,570
are independent of
each other, so we

420
00:25:44,570 --> 00:25:47,970
don't need to really wait
for all of these steps

421
00:25:47,970 --> 00:25:52,850
to be created
sequentially by the model,

422
00:25:52,850 --> 00:25:56,330
so we want to see how we can
leverage this parallelism

423
00:25:56,330 --> 00:25:57,530
observation.

424
00:25:57,530 --> 00:26:00,690
And so the idea is,
we want the model

425
00:26:00,690 --> 00:26:05,870
identify this parallelization
opportunities and act on it.

426
00:26:05,870 --> 00:26:11,370
So SPRINT is a
framework that does

427
00:26:11,370 --> 00:26:15,170
come in during the post-training
and/or fine tuning process,

428
00:26:15,170 --> 00:26:18,850
and what it does is that
it takes a reasoning model

429
00:26:18,850 --> 00:26:24,690
and enables the reasoning
model to execute the response

430
00:26:24,690 --> 00:26:25,810
generation.

431
00:26:25,810 --> 00:26:32,970
We are a planner that
creates a set of plans

432
00:26:32,970 --> 00:26:38,410
for how to approach solving a
problem and a set of executors,

433
00:26:38,410 --> 00:26:42,250
and those executors can be
run in parallel in order

434
00:26:42,250 --> 00:26:45,210
to carry out the plan.

435
00:26:45,210 --> 00:26:47,110
And this can happen
again and again.

436
00:26:47,110 --> 00:26:48,890
We can have the next slide.

437
00:26:48,890 --> 00:26:55,450
We can have a plan, a
bunch of execution plans

438
00:26:55,450 --> 00:26:57,710
that are carried
out in parallel,

439
00:26:57,710 --> 00:26:59,650
and then we can have
another set of plans

440
00:26:59,650 --> 00:27:04,130
and then another set
of parallel executions,

441
00:27:04,130 --> 00:27:08,610
so the interleaving of
these planning and execution

442
00:27:08,610 --> 00:27:12,250
is what it can enable
acceleration of the reasoning

443
00:27:12,250 --> 00:27:13,410
process.

444
00:27:13,410 --> 00:27:18,040
So let's see how we can
enable the model to create

445
00:27:18,040 --> 00:27:22,120
these parallel traces.

446
00:27:22,120 --> 00:27:24,280
And this is
something that I want

447
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:26,812
you to think about it
beyond just this paper,

448
00:27:26,812 --> 00:27:28,520
and we're seeing it
in the following work

449
00:27:28,520 --> 00:27:30,520
that we are talking about.

450
00:27:30,520 --> 00:27:37,760
A lot of times when we want
to create data and figure out

451
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:39,760
a certain behavior
in the model, we

452
00:27:39,760 --> 00:27:44,960
can use the LLMs itself in
the fine tuning data creation

453
00:27:44,960 --> 00:27:48,440
process, and here is
an example of that.

454
00:27:48,440 --> 00:27:54,660
Given a reasoning model's
response trajectory

455
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:58,200
to a question, we
can decompose it

456
00:27:58,200 --> 00:28:00,760
in a number of steps via an LLM.

457
00:28:00,760 --> 00:28:04,360
So in this case, for
example, we had DeepSeek-R1

458
00:28:04,360 --> 00:28:06,920
generate these reasoning
traces and responses

459
00:28:06,920 --> 00:28:08,340
to these questions.

460
00:28:08,340 --> 00:28:11,320
And then we had a
model like GPT-4o

461
00:28:11,320 --> 00:28:13,320
going through this
reasoning trace,

462
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:19,320
and annotate the steps of
the reasoning response.

463
00:28:19,320 --> 00:28:22,680
And not only that, for
each of these steps,

464
00:28:22,680 --> 00:28:28,280
we ask the GPT-4o to
annotate whether which

465
00:28:28,280 --> 00:28:31,520
part is the planning part, and
which part is the execution

466
00:28:31,520 --> 00:28:32,560
part.

467
00:28:32,560 --> 00:28:36,760
And in some steps, there were
multiple execution pieces

468
00:28:36,760 --> 00:28:40,400
for a given plan.

469
00:28:40,400 --> 00:28:43,460
So for example,
we had this query,

470
00:28:43,460 --> 00:28:45,280
we had the reasoning
trace, and then

471
00:28:45,280 --> 00:28:47,740
there are a bunch of things
that needs to be done.

472
00:28:47,740 --> 00:28:52,240
And then when the reasoning
stops, there is a final answer.

473
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:56,360
Then we use GPT-4o to
go through this process

474
00:28:56,360 --> 00:29:00,680
and create these
steps 1, 2, and k.

475
00:29:00,680 --> 00:29:03,060
Basically, now we have
these annotations,

476
00:29:03,060 --> 00:29:06,840
and within each
step, the model also

477
00:29:06,840 --> 00:29:09,860
annotates the plan
and the execution.

478
00:29:09,860 --> 00:29:11,590
Which part is the planning part?

479
00:29:11,590 --> 00:29:15,470
Which part is the
execution of that plan?

480
00:29:15,470 --> 00:29:21,050
Given that, then we can
create a DAG of these steps.

481
00:29:21,050 --> 00:29:23,970
So we have step 1 and
step 2 and all the way,

482
00:29:23,970 --> 00:29:26,970
and we can, again,
use the model.

483
00:29:26,970 --> 00:29:28,750
And the model does a
really good job here

484
00:29:28,750 --> 00:29:32,810
of saying whether how these
steps are related to each other.

485
00:29:32,810 --> 00:29:36,490
For example, step 4 and 2 are
not dependent on each other,

486
00:29:36,490 --> 00:29:40,150
but they both depend on step
1-- is a follow-up to step 1,

487
00:29:40,150 --> 00:29:45,070
so now we have a DAG of an
optimizer and basically,

488
00:29:45,070 --> 00:29:52,910
creating the DAG that describes
how this reasoning trace works.

489
00:29:52,910 --> 00:29:55,210
And then we can do
packing of these steps.

490
00:29:55,210 --> 00:29:58,010
For example, step 1
can be done first,

491
00:29:58,010 --> 00:30:01,070
and step 2 and 3 can be
done completely in parallel

492
00:30:01,070 --> 00:30:03,990
with each other and so on.

493
00:30:03,990 --> 00:30:12,030
And once we have these data
set that we have annotated,

494
00:30:12,030 --> 00:30:14,970
then LRM here is just a
