883
00:52:48,560 --> 00:52:52,880
Basically, the model takes a
lot of whatever it wants to do,

884
00:52:52,880 --> 00:52:55,020
generates a final
answer, and the reward

885
00:52:55,020 --> 00:52:58,100
is just based on the final
answer, whether it's correct

886
00:52:58,100 --> 00:53:02,260
or not, and then we back
propagate that towards actions.

887
00:53:02,260 --> 00:53:10,555
And here, we want to
have more of governance

888
00:53:10,555 --> 00:53:13,140
on the process of
generating these steps

889
00:53:13,140 --> 00:53:15,980
and how we can optimize them.

890
00:53:15,980 --> 00:53:23,300
So this framework called SWiRL,
and the design goal for SWiRL

891
00:53:23,300 --> 00:53:24,240
is the following.

892
00:53:24,240 --> 00:53:27,140
First of all, at
a high level, we

893
00:53:27,140 --> 00:53:31,050
want the model to solve complex
problem and multi-step reasoning

894
00:53:31,050 --> 00:53:32,530
task.

895
00:53:32,530 --> 00:53:36,610
We want the model to
know when to call a tool,

896
00:53:36,610 --> 00:53:39,910
generate the right
queries to invoke a tool,

897
00:53:39,910 --> 00:53:42,990
because we need to
know how to use a tool,

898
00:53:42,990 --> 00:53:47,210
the model needs to know that,
carry out these reasoning steps

899
00:53:47,210 --> 00:53:51,090
and maintain
accuracy across them,

900
00:53:51,090 --> 00:53:54,050
and learn how to
recover from an error

901
00:53:54,050 --> 00:53:59,130
and learn when to stop taking
new steps and calling new tools,

902
00:53:59,130 --> 00:54:03,950
and say, OK, I'm now ready
to generate a final answer,

903
00:54:03,950 --> 00:54:06,290
so we want the model
to know these steps.

904
00:54:06,290 --> 00:54:10,730
We also want to avoid using
tools during the training

905
00:54:10,730 --> 00:54:15,310
process, because like I
mentioned, this can be slow.

906
00:54:15,310 --> 00:54:20,890
Tools can fail, and can have
bugs, so we want to avoid that.

907
00:54:20,890 --> 00:54:23,830
And ideally, we want the
model to generalize this,

908
00:54:23,830 --> 00:54:26,970
so ideally, we want
the model to learn

909
00:54:26,970 --> 00:54:31,770
when to adapt to new tools
and new reasoning tasks

910
00:54:31,770 --> 00:54:34,530
and capabilities.

911
00:54:34,530 --> 00:54:40,890
So to approach this,
here is the process.

912
00:54:40,890 --> 00:54:48,650
The first step of this process
is that let's start with

913
00:54:48,650 --> 00:54:52,270
creating synthetic
multi-step data for a model,

914
00:54:52,270 --> 00:54:54,690
so given a prompt--

915
00:54:54,690 --> 00:54:58,730
and in order to really
annotate these steps,

916
00:54:58,730 --> 00:55:04,210
let's have the model
take one step at a time

917
00:55:04,210 --> 00:55:09,870
and let the model know at each
step that it's free to reason,

918
00:55:09,870 --> 00:55:13,810
create a chain of
thought, call a tool,

919
00:55:13,810 --> 00:55:15,890
or propose a final answer.

920
00:55:15,890 --> 00:55:21,410
So having an input prompt at
step 1, we tell the model,

921
00:55:21,410 --> 00:55:24,050
you have access to
these tools, you

922
00:55:24,050 --> 00:55:26,010
can generate a reasoning step.

923
00:55:26,010 --> 00:55:29,380
If you're ready to
generate the final answer,

924
00:55:29,380 --> 00:55:31,920
just say that and create that.

925
00:55:31,920 --> 00:55:37,740
So given this prompt, the model
would take the first action,

926
00:55:37,740 --> 00:55:41,540
which could be a reasoning
step followed by a tool call,

927
00:55:41,540 --> 00:55:44,460
and then we show the model
the environment response.

928
00:55:49,520 --> 00:55:55,140
So given that, then we show the
model again the same prompt,

929
00:55:55,140 --> 00:55:59,880
but this time, we have the
context from the previous step.

930
00:55:59,880 --> 00:56:03,380
So basically, we say, here
is the original prompt,

931
00:56:03,380 --> 00:56:05,400
here is the previous
action, and here is

932
00:56:05,400 --> 00:56:08,760
the result of taking that
action from the environment.

933
00:56:08,760 --> 00:56:12,800
You can take a new
action and call

934
00:56:12,800 --> 00:56:17,800
a tool, or if you're ready to
create a final answer, just

935
00:56:17,800 --> 00:56:19,480
output the final answer.

936
00:56:19,480 --> 00:56:25,040
So by design, through this
adapt to iterative prompting,

937
00:56:25,040 --> 00:56:27,920
we are creating this
multi-step data set,

938
00:56:27,920 --> 00:56:31,700
and the steps can
be from 1 to 3 or 5,

939
00:56:31,700 --> 00:56:34,060
and so on for different queries.

940
00:56:37,480 --> 00:56:40,420
Then we need a label.

941
00:56:40,420 --> 00:56:47,240
We want to have a notation
of how good this step is,

942
00:56:47,240 --> 00:56:51,720
and to do so, we ask
an LLM-as-a-Judge,

943
00:56:51,720 --> 00:56:55,220
given the prior context
and the current action,

944
00:56:55,220 --> 00:56:58,560
which is a reasoning step,
followed by a tool called,

945
00:56:58,560 --> 00:57:02,480
give us a reward function, or
give us your estimate of how

946
00:57:02,480 --> 00:57:06,860
good this trajectory
and final action is.

947
00:57:10,960 --> 00:57:14,840
Given that we have for each
query and we have a set

948
00:57:14,840 --> 00:57:20,840
of steps, and for each step, we
have a LLM-as-a-Judge score that

949
00:57:20,840 --> 00:57:22,760
is assigned to that step.

950
00:57:22,760 --> 00:57:24,450
And we can do that offline.

951
00:57:24,450 --> 00:57:27,830
We can go through many, many
questions in parallel offline

952
00:57:27,830 --> 00:57:33,670
and generate these steps
and labeled annotations.

953
00:57:33,670 --> 00:57:37,050
Once we have these
steps per prompt,

954
00:57:37,050 --> 00:57:39,930
we can filter this
data in different ways.

955
00:57:39,930 --> 00:57:43,870
For example, we can
only keep the data,

956
00:57:43,870 --> 00:57:50,750
where the LLM-as-a-Judge decided
every single step was a good

957
00:57:50,750 --> 00:57:51,930
step.

958
00:57:51,930 --> 00:57:53,870
Every single action was good.

959
00:57:53,870 --> 00:57:56,650
Or we can go based on
outcome filter data,

960
00:57:56,650 --> 00:58:00,270
and that would be we
keep all the trajectories

961
00:58:00,270 --> 00:58:03,070
that the final answer was
correct, no matter what

962
00:58:03,070 --> 00:58:04,650
the label per step was.

963
00:58:04,650 --> 00:58:08,210
And we're going to see how these
two differed with each other.

964
00:58:08,210 --> 00:58:14,350
But the whole intuition,
motivation behind this part

965
00:58:14,350 --> 00:58:17,830
is that, for these
reasoning prompts, we

966
00:58:17,830 --> 00:58:24,120
have a number of steps,
and then for each step,

967
00:58:24,120 --> 00:58:27,580
we have a reward created by
the model, which we can use

968
00:58:27,580 --> 00:58:30,060
or not use during our
training, but let's

969
00:58:30,060 --> 00:58:32,940
see how these two play out.

970
00:58:32,940 --> 00:58:37,620
Then having this data that
we have generated offline,

971
00:58:37,620 --> 00:58:40,080
we can start the
training process,

972
00:58:40,080 --> 00:58:42,580
and the training in
this part is done based

973
00:58:42,580 --> 00:58:44,280
on reinforcement learning.

974
00:58:44,280 --> 00:58:47,580
So maybe you can pay attention
to this figure on the right.

975
00:58:47,580 --> 00:58:50,320
This is a very simple example,
but let's go through that.

976
00:58:50,320 --> 00:58:54,380
Who is older, Glenn, I
don't know the last name,

977
00:58:54,380 --> 00:58:55,380
or Ross Lynch?

978
00:58:55,380 --> 00:58:57,400
So these two people,
who is older?

979
00:58:57,400 --> 00:59:01,980
So action 1 is to
figure out who is older.

980
00:59:01,980 --> 00:59:07,220
I should first search for
age of the first person.

981
00:59:07,220 --> 00:59:09,240
Then we have a reward
for this action.

982
00:59:09,240 --> 00:59:13,340
We ask the judge, LLM-as-a-Judge
to say given an input prompt

983
00:59:13,340 --> 00:59:18,680
and this action, what is
the reward for this step?

